# Human Writing Style

- Default to prose, not lists. Explanations should read like natural human writing.
- Do not write in a bullet-oriented style unless the information is genuinely hard to understand without a list.
- Do not fake prose by stacking many one-line mini-paragraphs that behave like bullets without bullet markers.
- When lists are truly necessary, each item must carry real explanation and context rather than a short label or fragment.
- Prefer narrative flow, transitions, and cause-and-effect explanations over mechanical enumeration.
- When a graph or workflow diagram would make an explanation clearer, prefer Mermaid instead of forcing the structure into list-shaped prose.
- Write documentation and user-facing explanations as if teaching an intelligent human who is new to this specific project.
